90: ## Evidence for Steps 3 to 7
91: 
92: For each step: the text that licenses a proposal, what to propose, and what does not license
93: one. `basis` is how the host sets `source`: `text-cue` is `llm-inferred` with
94: `rationale_world_fact` restating the cue, `rows` is `sample`, `library` is `common-sense`
95: citing `library_entry`. Library-basis constraints need no span (U5) but may carry one.
96: 
97: ### Step 3: combine entity types; derived or constrained
98: 
99: | Evidence | Propose | Not evidence |
100: |---|---|---|
101: | A definition equating two names: "the Insured (the Policyholder)", "X means Y", "also called" | Do not create two types. Use one id and put the other name in `description`. | Two names that merely co-occur in a sentence |
102: | Two types already in the model that the no-overlap test (below) flags | A consolidation proposal ([below](#the-consolidation-round)), never an in-round merge | |
103: | A defined term computed from other facts: "an eligible policy is one that is in force and unclaimed" | A structured derived fact type, only if the rule grammar in `representation-format.md` can express it | Arithmetic or aggregation: record it in `open_questions`; do not write an opaque rule |
104: 
105: ### Step 4: uniqueness and arity
280: }
281: ```
282: 
283: | Field | Rule |
284: |---|---|
285: | `quote` | A verbatim excerpt of the document. The host searches the NFC text; no match, or more than one without a `quote_occurrence` (0-based), drops the entry and logs it |
286: | `object_types`, `fact_types` | The model's own element shapes, ids that already exist are reused, `quote` becomes `provenance.spans` |
287: | `rows` | One entry per sample row; `quote` becomes the row's `sample_spans` entry (U6 needs every row named once) |
288: | `subtype_links` | Sets `subtype_of` and `inherits_reference` on the subtype, appends the span to its `provenance.spans`, puts `rationale` in `provenance.rationale` |
289: | `constraints` | `host` is a fact type id, or `model` for a top-level constraint. `constraint` is the constraint body as the [constraint reference](../../gnd-orm-core/references/constraint-reference.md) gives it, without `source`, `status`, `modality` or `provenance`. The host writes those: `text-cue` is `llm-inferred` with `rationale_world_fact` set to `cue`, `rows` is `sample`, `library` is `common-sense` with `library_entry`; always `proposed` and `deontic` |
290: | `derived_fact_types` | The model's fact type shape with `derivation: derived`, `storage: computed` and a structured `derivation_rule`, the object form that `representation-format.md` defines under derived fact types. `quote` is the sentence that defines the term and becomes `provenance.spans`; `rationale` goes to `provenance.rationale`. No `rows`: its facts follow from the rule, so it holds no sample. A rule the grammar cannot express is an `open_questions` entry, never a string rule |
291: | `open_questions` | Anything the evidence does not settle. Not written to the model; kept in the host's log for the owner |
292: 
293: ## Constraints on fact types that already hold facts
294: 
295: The procedure above prevents this by proposing constraints with the fact type. For a constraint
296: the text reveals only later, pass the population to the policy: `gnd evolve preview --policy
297: additive --check-population FILE`, and `apply` with the same options. The policy admits a new
298: constraint, or a change to one whose differing keys all strengthen, weaken or are admitted, when it is `proposed` and `deontic`, `FILE`
299: holds facts for every populated fact type it ranges over and none of them has sample rows, and
300: the constraint's own verdict over `FILE` is `pass`. The admission holds relative to `FILE`: the
301: constraint stays `proposed` and `deontic`, so it is advisory if a later population breaks it.

